<h1>Privacy</h1>

<p class="lead">
    This page summarises what MLCP reads, what it stores, where it stores it, and how to get rid
    of it. It is a plain-language summary, not a substitute for the data processing agreement.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">What we read from your Microsoft tenant</h2>

<table class="table align-middle">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col">Data</th>
            <th scope="col">Why</th>
            <th scope="col">Only if you grant</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Purchased licences, subscriptions and renewal dates</td>
            <td>To show what you own and when it renews</td>
            <td>Directory read consent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>User principal name, display name, department, whether the account is enabled, and which licences are assigned</td>
            <td>To show who has what, and to find seats assigned to disabled or inactive accounts</td>
            <td>Directory read consent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Service usage summaries per user</td>
            <td>To show which licensed services are actually used</td>
            <td>Usage reports consent, separately</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Azure consumption by subscription, resource group and meter</td>
            <td>To show Azure spend and forecast</td>
            <td>An Azure role you assign</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Billing transactions and invoice metadata</td>
            <td>To show what you actually pay per seat</td>
            <td>A billing role you assign</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 class="h5">What we never read</h2>
<p>
    Email, files, calendars, chat messages, Teams content, SharePoint documents, or anything else
    inside your users' mailboxes or drives. The permissions we request do not grant access to any
    of it, and cannot be widened without a new consent that an administrator has to approve.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">User names in usage reports</h2>
<p>
    Microsoft hides user names in usage reports by default. We leave that setting exactly as we
    find it. If your administrator chooses to turn it off, we record that they told us so, and you
    can tell us to stop using identifiable names at any time. We never change the setting for you.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">Tokens and credentials</h2>
<p>
    We never store your users' access or refresh tokens in our database. They live only in an
    encrypted distributed cache, protected with keys held in Azure Key Vault. Our own credential
    for calling Microsoft is a certificate in Key Vault, not a password, and we hold no credential
    capable of reading your data outside the permissions you granted.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">Where your data lives</h2>
<p>
    In the Azure region chosen when your organisation connected, and only there. We do not move a
    tenant between regions without asking. Each organisation's data is isolated at four levels,
    including row-level security enforced by the database itself, so a fault in application code
    cannot expose one customer's data to another.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">Getting your data out, and deleting it</h2>
<p>
    Any Owner can disconnect the organisation from the connection status page. Synchronisation
    stops immediately, and everything we hold is permanently deleted 30 days later. During those
    30 days you can reverse it. After deletion we retain only a dated record that the deletion
    happened, which contains no customer data.
</p>

<p>
    To revoke our access entirely, remove MLCP from
    <strong>Entra admin centre &rarr; Enterprise applications</strong>. Disconnecting stops us
    using the permissions; removing the application withdraws them.
</p>

